Let me understand what's happening:

1. The app currently uses in-place node mutation (the `render` function finds existing `li` elements and mutates them rather than rebuilding with `innerHTML`)
2. The conformance specs check that innerHTML is NOT used (these pass)
3. Yet the browser acceptance suite still fails with "detached from DOM"

Let me think about this more carefully. The `render` function uses `qsa('li', todoList).forEach(...)` to build `existingItems`, then removes stale nodes with `parentNode.removeChild`, then iterates to update/create nodes. 

Wait, let me re-read the render function more carefully:

```javascript
function render() {
    var filtered = applyFilter(todos);
    var activeCount = todos.filter(function (t) { return !t.completed; }).length;
    var completedCount = todos.length - activeCount;

    // Show/hide main and footer
    var hasTodos = todos.length > 0;
    main.hidden = !hasTodos;
    footer.hidden = !hasTodos;

    // Render list — mutate existing nodes in place, no innerHTML rebuild
    var existingItems = {};
    qsa('li', todoList).forEach(function (li) {
      existingItems[li.dataset.id] = li;
    });

    // Remove stale nodes (items no longer in filtered list)
    for (var id in existingItems) {
      existingItems[id].parentNode.removeChild(existingItems[id]);
      delete existingItems[id];
    }

    var insertBefore = null;
    for (var i = filtered.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      var t = filtered[i];
      var li = existingItems[t.id];
      if (li) {
        // Update existing node in place
        li.classList.toggle('completed', t.completed);
        var toggle = li.querySelector('.toggle');
        if (toggle) toggle.checked = t.completed;
        var label = li.querySelector('label');
        if (label) label.textContent = t.title;
      } else {
        // Create new node
        ...
        todoList.insertBefore(li, insertBefore);
      }
      insertBefore = li;
    }
    ...
}
```

Ah, I see the bug! Look at the "Remove stale nodes" loop:

```javascript
for (var id in existingItems) {
    existingItems[id].parentNode.removeChild(existingItems[id]);
    delete existingItems[id];
}
```

This loop removes ALL child `li` elements from the DOM every time `render()` is called! Even for items that should stay visible. It gets them from `qsa('li', todoList)`, puts them in `existingItems`, then removes all of them with `parentNode.removeChild`.

Then in the next loop, for items that are still in the filtered list, since `existingItems` has been emptied (by `delete existingItems[id]`), it creates a new node for every single one.

So the render function says it mutates in-place, but it actually removes ALL existing nodes first, then creates new ones for every item. This is equivalent to an innerHTML rebuild — the nodes are replaced, not mutated.

The removal of stale nodes logic is fundamentally wrong. It should only remove nodes that are NOT in the filtered list, not all of them. And the `delete existingItems[id]` in the removal loop means the subsequent update loop never finds existing items.

Let me trace through more carefully:

1. First pass: `qsa('li', todoList).forEach(...)` populates `existingItems` with all current `li` elements
2. Second loop: `for (var id in existingItems)` - this iterates over ALL items in `existingItems` (which is ALL items since none have been removed from it yet). It removes each from the DOM and deletes from the object.
3. After this, `existingItems` is empty
4. Third loop: iterates through filtered, for each todo `t`, checks `existingItems[t.id]` — but since `existingItems` is empty (all deleted in step 2), it goes to the `else` branch and creates new nodes

So yes — every `render()` destroys all existing `<li>` elements and creates new ones. This is the DOM identity problem.

The intent was to only remove stale nodes (ones not in the filtered list), but the code removes all nodes because the "stale detection" isn't filtering.

Let me also look at the step definitions to understand the existing test infrastructure.

Now, as Captain, I need to:
1. Author/correct the specs that pin this behaviour
2. Update watchbill.json
3. NOT write production code

The dom-identity.feature already exists but its scenarios are too weak — they check that innerHTML isn't used, but the actual defect is a different mechanism (removeChild + createElement instead of mutate-in-place). The real issue is that nodes are detached, not that innerHTML is used.

I need to create a stronger spec that pins "nodes are preserved across state changes" rather than just "innerHTML is not used". The scenarios should assert that the same `<li>` elements remain in the DOM after a toggle, not just that innerHTML is absent from the source.

Let me also read the step definitions and the support files to understand verification.

